{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"body":"NEAR-MISS: THE BALLOT BUILDER SILENTLY EXCLUDED REBAR FROM THE 4-ARM BALLOTS. ballot.py carried ARM_ORDER with rebar first and full rebar support - top-band rule, self-qualification normalisation, provenance hashing - so the arm appeared wired. But one line dropped it: order = [a for a in ARM_ORDER if a != rebar] if wave == 3. That was correct for the nine three-arm runs and wrong for these two. The first build produced ballots of 49 and 72 items containing only ce, sp and mini; had it not been checked, six judges would have been dispatched against a ballot with no rebar on it and the entire point of the exercise - rebar judged in the same pass, not as a later remediation - would have been lost while every surface reported success. Caught by asserting the arm composition of the built artifact rather than trusting the build to have done what the code appeared to support. Fixed with an OPT-IN --four-arm flag rather than by changing the default, because the nine existing ballots are frozen and their adjudications pin ballot_sha256; regression-checked by rebuilding all nine without the flag and confirming 9 of 9 byte-identical. Rebuilt with the flag: carbon 64 items (rebar 15, ce 26, sp 13, mini 10), uswds 92 items (rebar 20, ce 43, sp 20, mini 9). Same defect family as the other glob and predicate failures this session - a filter that was right for the old case and silently wrong for the new one."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789445691818807001,"uuid":"18c8c0cd-695a-4cd1-9900-5dee1a3a52c4"}